[
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Shall there be a change to the Colorado Revised Statutes creating new law to increase water and land conservation funding without raising taxes, and, in connection therewith, through a voter-approved revenue change, allowing the state to keep and spend a portion of revenue from the state sales tax on sporting goods and equipment to conserve and protect Colorado's water, land, and forests, prevent wildfires, support outdoor recreation training and activities, and reduce revenue spent on these conservation purposes if necessary to preserve funding for certain tax credits?",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Who put the measure on the ballot?",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-heading",
    "version": 1,
    "tag": "h2"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Oppose. Proposition 137 was placed on the ballot through a citizen initiative backed by a group called “Protect Colorado’s Land and Water, Prevent Wildfires.”",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Short Description:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-heading",
    "version": 1,
    "tag": "h2"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Allows the state to retain specified sales-tax revenue from sporting goods and equipment and direct it to conservation purposes including wildfire prevention, land and water projects, and outdoor recreation, rather than refunding that revenue under TABOR.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Analysis and Commentary:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-heading",
    "version": 1,
    "tag": "h2"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Oppose. Proposition 137 was placed on the ballot through a citizen initiative backed by a group called “Protect Colorado’s Land and Water, Prevent Wildfires.”",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Proposition 137 is a well-intentioned measure, with some useful investments in state outdoors priorities. However, I do not support the measure because its passage would exempt the funding from TABOR, which places this funding above priorities like health care, education, and human services.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Here’s where Proposition 137 would do, according to the Colorado Blue Book:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "47.5% - Great Outdoors Colorado (GOCO). About $83.1 million would be dedicated to GOCO, an organization established in the state constitution that funds parks, trails, and wildlife projects through grants to local governments, the state, and other conservation efforts. GOCO is funded by the state lottery, amounting to about $90 million annually, and currently receives no state funding from taxes.",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 1
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "47.5% - Wildfire risk mitigation, forest management, and watershed restoration projects. About $83.1 million would be dedicated to these efforts per year. In budget year 2027-28, a one-time investment of $10.0 million would be made out of the total funding available to pay for damages that may arise from the use of prescribed fires. These wildfire risk mitigation and forest management efforts currently receive about $16.4 million annually from the state, which is largely awarded through grants from state agencies for fuels reduction in forests and other risk mitigation projects near developed areas. Watershed restoration projects and water projects reducing risk of post-wildfire floods currently receive about $5 million annually from the state.",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 2
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 0,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "5% - Outdoor recreation, economic development, and equity programs. The remaining revenue, about $8.7 million, would be dedicated to outdoor recreation and equity programs funded through the Colorado Office of Economic Development and International Trade and Colorado Parks and Wildlife. These programs currently receive about $800,000 from the state annually. The funding is used to support outdoor industry business advancement and outdoor recreation planning for rural communities, and to provide grants to organizations and projects improving access and representation for youth and their families in the Colorado outdoors.",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "listitem",
        "version": 1,
        "value": 3
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "list",
    "version": 1,
    "listType": "bullet",
    "start": 1,
    "tag": "ul"
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Proposition 137, Colorado Blue Book",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://bouldercoloradovoterguide.com/content/files/2026/09/proposition-137-direct-sporting-goods-sales-tax-revenue-for-conservation.pdf"
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0.8,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "According to the Colorado Blue Book, “The measure will decrease the amount refunded to taxpayers in years when state revenue is over the TABOR limit,” which is the expected outcome of dedicated spending. However, that same dedicated funding mechanism will negatively affect spending we have dedicated for general fund priorities like healthcare, childcare, and education. “In years where state revenue is below the TABOR limit, the measure does not impact TABOR refunds, but it may reduce the amount of money available for other general budget priorities.”",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "The effect would be compounded with if we pass Proposition NN:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Proposition NN allows the state to keep and spend revenue that would otherwise be refunded to taxpayers under TABOR, up to a newly established limit. Because Proposition 137 reduces revenue subject to the constitutional revenue limit, ",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 1,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "Proposition 137 will reduce the amount of money the state actually keeps and spends under Proposition NN, if both measures pass.",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "Proposition 137, Colorado Blue Book",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://bouldercoloradovoterguide.com/content/files/2026/09/proposition-137-direct-sporting-goods-sales-tax-revenue-for-conservation.pdf"
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "extended-quote",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "A few groups that might be otherwise supportive (like the Colorado Democratic Party and Boulder Progressives), are opposed to the measure because of the spending effects. Per the Colorado Sun:",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      }
    ],
    "direction": "ltr",
    "format": "",
    "indent": 0,
    "type": "paragraph",
    "version": 1
  },
  {
    "children": [
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": "No committee has formed to oppose Proposition 137, but the League of Women Voters ",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
            "text": "opposes the ballot measure",
            "type": "extended-text",
            "version": 1
          }
        ],
        "direction": "ltr",
        "format": "",
        "indent": 0,
        "type": "link",
        "version": 1,
        "rel": null,
        "target": null,
        "title": null,
        "url": "https://lwvcolorado.org/content.aspx?page_id=22&club_id=314195&module_id=747768"
      },
      {
        "detail": 0,
        "format": 0,
        "mode": "normal",
        "style": "",
        "text": ", saying the “permanent transfer” of funds from the “perpetually short” state general fund, “",
        "type": "extended-text",
        "version": 1
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "detail": 0,
            "format": 8,
            "mode": "normal",
            "style": "",
